    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d227">

                <pb facs="0405" n="309" sameAs="frus:frus1931-41v01/pg309"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.3362i/16: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-08-07T19:00:00-04:00">August 7, 1936—7
                    p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>102. Embassy’s telegram No. 163, July 28, 1 p.m. With regard to the suggestion
                    made relative to the <hi rendition="#italic">Alden</hi>, the Department assumes
                    that there is no prospect that the Japanese authorities will take favorable
                    action. In reply to a communication from the Japanese Embassy here,<anchor
                        n="47" xml:id="d227-f1"/> the Department is today returning an adverse
                        answer<anchor n="47" xml:id="d227-f2"/> to that Embassy’s request that the
                    Japanese Government training ship <hi rendition="#italic">Shintoku Maru</hi> be
                    permitted to enter a Hawaiian harbor which is not listed as a port of entry.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
